validated_work_order.approach:
One behaviour fix plus three test/comment cleanups, all in the two files #63 touched.

1. The ingest fix (src/api/routes.js). Split the two questions apart rather than tuning the fallback. Keep resolveTenantCurrency() exactly as it is — it is correct for the read side (GET /v1/metrics) and its doc comment says so. Add a sibling helper for the write side that resolves to null and branches, and point line 160 at it:

  function tenantCurrencyMismatch(row, spendCurrency) {
    const canonical = canonicalCurrency(row?.currency);
    return canonical === null ? row.currency !== spendCurrency : canonical !== spendCurrency;
  }

A stored code that names an ISO currency is compared canonically, so 'usd' and ' USD ' accept the USD spend they name. A stored code that names none is compared VERBATIM, so it never matches a validated spend currency and the existing 400 fires with the stored string in the message and details, exactly as on main. Note that a validated spend currency is always in ISO_CURRENCIES (measurement.js rejects anything else before this point), so the verbatim branch can never accidentally accept: for 'ZZZ' it is always a mismatch. The message and details blocks are untouched, so the operator signal and the existing ZZZ+USD / USD+EUR tests are unaffected.

2. money() (src/web/pages.js:240). Take the reviewer's first option and revert the expression to main's exact form, `formatMoney(fromMicros(micros, ISO_CURRENCIES.includes(currency) ? currency : 'INR'))`, restoring the ISO_CURRENCIES import alongside canonicalCurrency. Then correct the doc comment, which currently makes a claim this change makes false: the paragraph at 222-228 says mis-cased codes render as dollars 'because canonicalCurrency resolves a stored code before the membership test'. After the revert money() no longer resolves anything — tenantCurrency() does, upstream, at every call site. The claim stays TRUE of the render, but the stated mechanism becomes false, so reword it to attribute the case-resolution to the tenantCurrency() seam above and to say plainly that no test can pin money()'s own guard, because every caller has already resolved the code. Do not delete the guard: it is main's line, it keeps a page render from throwing on a code that reaches it by a future path, and deleting it is a strictly larger diff for no reachable behaviour change.

3. The test fixtures. Fold usdCasingRepos() into usdRepos() by giving usdRepos an optional stored-currency parameter defaulting to 'USD', reusing the single opportunity_id 'opp_usd_expensive', and rewriting the 7 usdCasingRepos(...) call sites to usdRepos(...). The doc comment moves with it. Separately, convert the three inline Qualified CPL extractions (374-375, 412-413, 1076-1077) to qualifiedCplValue(html), which is what makes that extraction actually buy something. Both are pure refactors: no assertion changes, so AC-6's greps and every currency assertion hold as written.

I prototyped items 1 and 2 and ran the full suite plus a purpose-built test for all five currency directions (ZZZ+INR, ZZZ+USD, usd+USD, ' USD '+USD, usd+EUR) before writing this order. Results are in evidence[].
